STEP 2 -- is BGPv4 service-gated on this build? (measured both ways)      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: OSPFv2 and RIP on this same software are strictly gated by `service ospf`
  and `service rip`. If BGP were gated the same way, a later refusal could be
  mistaken for a platform gap, so the question is settled first-hand and
  constructively, in both directions.

  (a) bgpd is ALREADY RUNNING at baseline, with no `service bgp` in any
      running-config, on both an atlnz-licensed x960-56 and a Base-licensed x960-28:
swi_d# show process | include bgp
  1721 bgpd  3  0.0  0.1  20 sleep
swi_b# show process | include bgp
  2281 bgpd  3  0.0  0.5  20 sleep
swi_b# show running-config | include service
  service password-encryption / service ssh / no service telnet / service http /
  no service dhcp-server                   <-- no bgp line of any kind
      And the show commands ANSWER instead of refusing:
swi_d# show ip bgp summary
  (empty output, no `%` line)
swi_d# show ip bgp
  (empty output, no `%` line)

  (b) `router bgp` with NO `service bgp` -- accepted immediately (22:51:15):
awplus# configure terminal
  Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
awplus(config)# router bgp 65001
awplus(config-router)#                     <-- accepted 0.8 s after entering config
                                               mode: no refusal, no wait
awplus(config-router)# end
      Read back at 22:51:30:
awplus# show running-config router bgp
  !
  router bgp 65001
   bgp graceful-restart
  !
awplus# show running-config | include bgp
  router bgp 65001 / bgp graceful-restart  <-- NO `service bgp` line was added
awplus# show process | include bgp
  1721 bgpd                                <-- the SAME pid: `router bgp` did not
                                               start a daemon, it was already up

  (c) what `service bgp` actually is on this build, probed read-only with
      tools/qmark.py (`?` completion, never the command plus a carriage return):
awplus(config)# service ?
  ...  bgp    Enable BGP service modes  ...
awplus(config)# service bgp ?
  evpn  Enable EVPN
      and the bare command is incomplete (22:52:18):
awplus(config)# service bgp
  % Incomplete command.
awplus(config)# do show running-config | include service
  service ssh / no service telnet / service http / no service rip / no service ospf /
  no service dhcp-server                   <-- unchanged; nothing was added

  => STEP 2 PASS: measured in both directions, BGPv4 on this build is NOT
     service-gated. bgpd runs from boot on both units with no `service bgp` in any
     configuration, `router bgp <asn>` is accepted with no preparation at all, and
     the only completion of `service bgp` is `evpn` -- the bare form being
     `% Incomplete command.` BGPv4 therefore behaves differently from OSPFv2 and RIP
     on the same software. No later step in this case may be read as a platform gap
     on the strength of a service-gate message, because no such gate exists here.

