                                               the static. Two different "best": BGP's
                                               own path selection picks the best BGP
                                               path; the RIB then compares that path's
                                               AD (20) against every other protocol's.
                                               `Advertised to non peer-group peers:
                                               10.0.41.2` -- the DUT re-advertises to
                                               swi_a what it learned from swi_b.

  Leg L3-static-over-bgp, same four flows, same arguments:
  cap L3-static-over-bgp-eth3.pcap: 39 frames
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 10.70.70.1
    x5  ICMP t11/c0  10.0.41.2 -> 192.168.10.5            <-- TIME EXCEEDED, source = swi_a
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 10.90.90.1
    x5  ICMP t0/c0  10.90.90.1   -> 192.168.10.5          <-- SUBJECT-A still 5/5
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9   (0 replies)
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1
    x5  ICMP t0/c0  192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5          <-- instrument 5/5
    x2  ICMP t3/c0  192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5          <-- the control's unreachables, from the DUT

    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, 0 echo replies, and 5 ICMP TIME-EXCEEDED from 10.0.41.2
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  THE ATTRIBUTION. Every one of the five frames came back as ICMP type 11 sourced
  from 10.0.41.2, which is swi_a's vlan41 address. Before the static they came back
  as echo replies from 10.70.70.1, which is swi_b. So the traffic demonstrably left
  by a DIFFERENT PORT, to a DIFFERENT DEVICE. Type 11 rather than type 3 because the
  DUT re-advertises 10.70.70.1/32 to swi_a over eBGP (printed above), so swi_a's own
  best route for it points back at the DUT: the frames loop DUT -> swi_a -> DUT until
  the TTL runs out, and swi_a is the hop that zeroes it. Only swi_a can source that,
  and TTL responses are not rate-limited, so 5 of 5 is a count that means something.

  Static removed again at 22:58:51, and the BGP route returns -- proven on the wire:
awplus(config)# no ip route 10.70.70.1/32 10.0.41.2
awplus# show ip route 10.70.70.1
  Routing entry for 10.70.70.1/32
    Known via "bgp", distance 20, metric 0, best
    * via 10.0.61.2, vlan61
awplus# show running-config | include ip route
  (EMPTY again)

  Leg L4-static-removed, cap 37 frames:
    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies   (back from swi_b)
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  => STEP 5 PASS: eBGP administrative distance 20 behaves as documented. A static at
     AD 1 takes the prefix out of the FIB -- and the egress port MOVES, attributed on
     the wire by ICMP sourced from the other partner -- and removing the static hands
     the prefix straight back to BGP, with forwarding restored to the original
     partner in the same four-flow leg.

STEP 6 -- the protocol on the wire is BGP-4, decoded                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: prove that what carried these prefixes was BGP-4 itself, and not something
  else that happens to produce a route with the letter B beside it. No `show` command
  can establish that. Two instruments were used.

6a. PORT MIRROR of the eBGP-facing port to the second host NIC.
awplus(config)# interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)# mirror interface port1.0.16 direction receive
awplus(config-if)# shutdown
awplus(config-if)# no shutdown
awplus# show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.48
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.16
awplus# show interface port1.0.48 status
  port1.0.48 connected vlan 48 a-full a-1000

    The mirror was applied at 23:00:33 and removed at 23:06:54. Read back from the
    capture on tb277 eth4:
host# sudo tcpdump -r /tmp/ckD/T43858/mirror179.pcap -nn -q | head -3
  23:03:43.814073 IP 10.70.70.1 > 192.168.10.5: ICMP echo reply, id 0, seq 0, length 37
  23:03:44.113084 IP 10.70.70.1 > 192.168.10.5: ICMP echo reply, id 0, seq 0, length 37
  23:03:44.408002 IP 10.70.70.1 > 192.168.10.5: ICMP echo reply, id 0, seq 0, length 37
host# sudo tcpdump -r /tmp/ckD/T43858/mirror179.pcap -nn -q "tcp port 179"
  23:03:56.104152 IP 10.0.61.2.33334 > 10.0.61.1.179: tcp 0
  23:03:56.104187 IP 10.0.61.2.179  > 10.0.61.1.49698: tcp 0
  23:03:56.104222 IP 10.0.61.2.52924 > 10.0.61.1.179: tcp 0
  23:03:56.104665 IP 10.0.61.2.179  > 10.0.61.1.49698: tcp 72      <-- the OPEN exchange
  23:04:19.105599 IP 10.0.61.2.179  > 10.0.61.1.49698: tcp 48      <-- an UPDATE
  23:04:46.105942 IP 10.0.61.2.179  > 10.0.61.1.49698: tcp 19      <-- a KEEPALIVE
  (14 TCP/179 packets in all)

    Every one of those timestamps falls inside the mirror's own 23:00:33-23:06:54
    window, and every one is a frame INGRESSING port1.0.16, which is exactly what
    `direction receive` must copy: the mirrored ICMP echo replies from swi_b, then
    the mirrored BGP teardown and re-establishment caused by
    `clear ip bgp 10.0.61.2`. The capture confirms that BGP runs between
    10.0.61.2 and 10.0.61.1 on TCP port 179. It cannot decode the message bodies,
    so the protocol verdict rests on 6b.

6b. THE DUT'S OWN `tcpdump`, at privileged exec. BGP is unicast TCP between two
    router addresses and is therefore invisible to a host NIC on another segment, so
    it is captured where it actually runs. The session reset was triggered from
    swi_b's console while the DUT's console ran the capture -- two different devices,
    one console driver each.

    (1) THE OPEN EXCHANGE. On swi_b: `clear ip bgp 10.0.61.1`. On the DUT:
swi_d# tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 14 -nn -v tcp port 179
  10.0.61.2.59396 > 10.0.61.1.179: Flags [S] ...
  10.0.61.1.179 > 10.0.61.2.59396: Flags [S.] ...
  10.0.61.1.50428 > 10.0.61.2.179: Flags [S] ...        <-- BOTH ends initiate: a
                                                            textbook BGP connection
                                                            collision
  10.0.61.2.59396 > 10.0.61.1.179: length 53: BGP
    Open Message (1), length: 53
      Version 4, my AS 65002, Holdtime 90s, ID 2.2.2.2
      Optional parameters, length: 24
        Option Capabilities Advertisement (2), length: 6
          Multiprotocol Extensions (1), length: 4
            AFI IPv4 (1), SAFI Unicast (1)
        ...
          32-Bit AS Number (65), length: 4
             4 Byte AS 65002
  10.0.61.1.179 > 10.0.61.2.59396: length 72: BGP
    Open Message (1), length: 53
      Version 4, my AS 65001, Holdtime 90s, ID 1.1.1.1
      ... AFI IPv4 (1), SAFI Unicast (1) ... 4 Byte AS 65001
    Keepalive Message (4), length: 19
                                           <-- Version 4: this is BGP-4, which is what
                                               the case is named for. The DUT's own AS
                                               65001 and router-id 1.1.1.1 on the wire,
                                               the default Holdtime 90 s, the IPv4
                                               Unicast AFI/SAFI capability and the
                                               4-byte-AS capability. TCP port 179,
                                               TTL 1 (direct eBGP peering),
                                               TOS 0xc0 (CS6 internetwork control).

    (2) THE RESET, in protocol terms:
  10.0.61.2.179 > 10.0.61.1.49698: length 21: BGP
    Notification Message (3), length: 21, Cease (6), subcode Other Configuration Change (6)
  ... then FIN, then a fresh TCP SYN.
                                           <-- `clear ip bgp <neighbor>` is a HARD
                                               reset: an explicit NOTIFICATION with
                                               cease subcode "Other Configuration
                                               Change", the TCP connection closed and a
                                               new one opened. Not a silent drop.
      Measured from `show ip bgp summary` either side of it: the session was
      Established again in ~6 s and the prefix was re-received and back in the FIB
      within ~24 s, while the OTHER neighbour's session showed Up/Down 00:11:29
      straight through -- an untouched live control for the reset.

    (3) THE UPDATE THAT CARRIES THE PREFIX, captured during the re-advertisement of
        STEP 7:
swi_d# tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 1 -nn -v tcp port 179 and greater 90
  10:11:39.461215  10.0.61.2.50078 > 10.0.61.1.179 ... length 48: BGP
    Update Message (2), length: 48
      Origin (1), length: 1, Flags [T]: IGP
      AS Path (2), length: 6, Flags [T]: 65002
      Next Hop (3), length: 4, Flags [T]: 10.0.61.2
      Updated routes:
        10.70.70.1/32
                                           <-- the single most direct piece of evidence
                                               in the case: 10.70.70.1/32 reached the
                                               DUT inside a BGP UPDATE message, with
                                               ORIGIN IGP, AS_PATH {65002} and
                                               NEXT_HOP 10.0.61.2. No `show` command
                                               can produce that, and no other routing
                                               protocol was running that could have
                                               carried it.

    (4) THE WITHDRAW, captured during the withdrawal of STEP 7, same filter:
  10:13:05.464158  10.0.61.2.50078 > 10.0.61.1.179 ... length 28: BGP
    Update Message (2), length: 28
      Withdrawn routes:
        10.70.70.1/32
      End-of-Rib Marker (empty NLRI)
                                           <-- BGP withdraws EXPLICITLY, in one UPDATE.
                                               "End-of-Rib Marker" is tcpdump's label
                                               for an UPDATE carrying no NLRI and no
                                               path attributes; it is not a separate
                                               message.

    BPF note for a repeat of this measurement: on this tcpdump, `greater N` counts the
    14-byte Ethernet header, so a 19-byte BGP Keepalive is an 85-byte frame.
    `greater 90` is the threshold that isolates UPDATEs (announce 114 B, withdraw
    94 B) from keepalives and bare ACKs.

  => STEP 6 PASS: BGP-4 is proven to be the protocol on the wire, decoded from the
     DUT's own capture of its own peering interface -- an OPEN Message announcing
     Version 4 and the DUT's AS 65001 and router-id 1.1.1.1; an UPDATE Message
     carrying the subject prefix 10.70.70.1/32 with ORIGIN IGP, AS_PATH {65002} and
     NEXT_HOP 10.0.61.2, which is what attributes the prefix to eBGP from AS 65002
     and which no `show` command could establish; an UPDATE explicitly withdrawing
     that prefix; and a NOTIFICATION with cease subcode Other Configuration Change on
     reset. The mirrored capture independently places BGP on TCP/179 between the two
     router addresses.

